cff-version: 1.2.0
message: Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its source, programs and certificates.
type: software
title: "Sharp embezzlement laws for quantum chains and finite Bell tests"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: '2026-11-11'
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["entanglement embezzlement", "critical spin chains", "XX chain", "Ising chain", "Schmidt spectrum", "nonlocal games", "Bell tests", "type III factors"]
abstract: "For the finite open critical XX and Ising chains, we prove a sharp entanglement-embezzlement law from their exact discrete Schmidt spectra, including all degeneracies. For a maximally entangled target of fixed dimension d, the optimal squared local-unitary error is E_d = (ln d)^2/(4S) + O_d(S^-2), where S is the half-chain entanglement entropy. A counting criterion gives a Gaussian crossover for growing d and the same sharp law for the XXZ corner-transfer-matrix spectrum as Δ decreases to 1. We compare the optimal loss in a fixed Bell game with 243 question pairs to the Bell-pair embezzlement error with explicit constants, and prove the loss floor (3−2√2)/243 for maximally entangled states of every dimension. With a shared nine-dimensional maximally entangled register, the open XX ground state at N = 2^19 beats this floor in the original game. At N = 2^84 the chain alone suffices. A tagged test gives an exact tilted CHSH separation for the four-spin XX ground state, with classical play in the original game branch. For the infinite critical XX ground state, the half-chain factors are type III_1 and the game value is one without attainment."
